This guide explains three independent computational validators:
K8. A: comparison of two separately implemented operator paths.
K8. B: exact relational invariance through repeated binary-scale expansion.
K8. C: finite Base-10 projection of established rational states followed by exact rational reconstruction.
It identifies their executed scopes, evidence, recorded results and validation boundaries.The programs are self-contained. They require no master program and do not consume one another's output files.Their assigned controls concern operator identity, binary-scale invariance and representation preservation. They do not rerun or extend the K.7 positional genealogy.K8. A compares two separately implemented paths identified as P.B.F. and Binary Pulse.It tests whether they produce the same complete executed state throughout the tested domain by comparing:
complete candidate fields;
complete projected-hit structures;
state classification at every tested position;
complete closure sequences;
observation, closure and reproduced-position counts;
the location of any first deviation.
Equal totals alone do not establish executed operator identity.The recorded execution uses the limit:Prime-reference information is neither used nor reported by K8. A.K8. B.py, version 2.1.0, tests these rational families:together with complementary closure:Execution begins at level zero. Numerators and denominators are doubled at each following level.At each level, the program verifies:
exact quotient equality;
the three-quarter-to-one-quarter ratio;
a shared denominator;
binary denominator growth;
complete complementary closure.
Between adjacent levels, it verifies that the numerators and denominators of all three families are multiplied by two.The written fractions expand while their rational values remain unchanged.All status-bearing calculations use fractions.Fraction. Floating-point arithmetic and numerical tolerance do not determine the result.K8. C tests whether established rational states can be represented as finite Base-10 strings and reconstructed as their original exact fractions without value displacement or information loss.The operation is:Base 10 is a downstream representation layer. It does not generate or modify the source relations.K8. C.py constructs exact Fraction representations of the established rational values at each binary-scale level before projection.Each exact fractional state is then encoded as a Base-10 string. The string is decoded into an exact Fraction and compared with the source value.The validator checks that:
every exact fraction reconstructs without loss;
equivalent binary-scale expressions produce the same Base-10 representation; remains exact;
Base-10 encoding introduces no new rational state.
The validator uses reduced exact rational values whose denominators permit terminating Base-10 expansions.Floating-point arithmetic and numerical tolerance do not determine the result.Paths below are relative to K.8 CODE ARCHITECTURE.Each package contains recorded evidence in its 2. OUTPUT folder:Each package's 3. FINAL REPORTS folder contains the corresponding report copy.The local package READMEs provide execution instructions and output-location details.The source defines the implemented operations. The recorded output establishes what was executed and reported.The combined K.8 result requires all three independent programs to report their required results.These remain three separate computational controls. Their combined PASS does not make them one operation.The three inspection questions are:
Operator: Do the separately implemented paths produce the same complete tested state, including fields, hits, classifications, closure sequences and counts?
Scale: Do the defined rational values remain exact while their fractional representations expand through binary scaling?
Representation: Do the defined rational states retain exact identity through finite Base-10 encoding and rational reconstruction?
Inspect the executable implementations and their recorded evidence for the stated scopes and conditions. Counts alone are insufficient for the operator-identity control.The validators operate on established relations. They do not independently derive their conceptual origin or introduce P.B.F. as an external formula applied to another program's output.Their recorded results do not extend the K.7 genealogy or establish unexecuted domains.K8. C tests representation preservation for the defined rational states; it does not derive the Base-10 number system.The K.8 results are limited to the executed controls. They do not independently determine the Riemann Hypothesis or perform the formal final review.Rp9 black 📁 RP9. THE RIEMANN SUBMISSION
